  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 AM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThis weekend:\n\nSurface low pressure is moving well off to the SE this morning as it \nexits IA. Over in our neck of the woods we have a trailing cold \nfront sliding across the region. Behind this front we can see \nclearing skies and northerly winds starting to filter in from \nCanada. Through the day some diurnally driven cumulus will form with \na larger cluster likely over the Arrowhead. Aided by some orographic \nlift and a shortwave moving aloft this could spawn some isolated \nshowers in the afternoon.  This activity will quickly taper off \nafter sunset. \n\nTemperatures for the weekend will be much milder with highs close to \nseasonal averages around upper 70s to low 80s. On shore flow during \nthe overnight period will also help to dip overnight lows into the \n50s. \n\nMonday/Tuesday:\n\nHigh pressure will be shunted off to the east as our next weather \nsystem begins its approach on Monday. Southerly flow out ahead of \nthis system will lead to warming temperatures with highs climbing \ninto the 80s. Dew points will also climb back into the mid and upper \n60s across out west. The 00Z suite of deterministic guidance \nhighlights a deep upper level low over central Canada with a strong \nupper level jet cruising from the Northern Rockies into this system. \nFarther south, an additional low pressure may develop in lee of\nthe Rockies on Monday but our main focus will be on the cold \nfront as it marches across the Northern Plains. Isentropic lift \nacross the region ahead of the cold front could lead to some \nisolated showers and storms Monday afternoon, but the best \nforcing associated with the front arrives Monday evening. \nConvective initiation will likely be to our west so we will have\nto monitor the line as it advances into our region. Instability\nmay be a bit limited based on how cloud cover and afternoon \nstorms develop. Additionally, the better shear will be tied to \nalong the front. For now, the severe risk looks marginal with \nmost of the NWP hatching out a 15% chance of severe in our NW \nwhich aligns well with what SPC has outlined. \n\nThe front will march through the region on Tuesday with rain and \nscattered thunderstorms expected. Convective parameter space at this \ntime does not look favorable for severe weather on Tuesday. Rain \nchances taper off in the evening and with the passage of the front \nthe region can expect temperatures to fall back into the 70s. \n\nMid week:\n\nHigh pressure over the region for Wednesday with temperatures \nstaying in the 70s. Temperatures will slowly increase through \nthe end of the work week with intermittent rain chances possible\nas the upper level pattern turns more zonal.",
